#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133503 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133948 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9017b4e-ddb3-56f6-ba30-b494a7e4e09f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Orthogonal Matrices"}] :question/id #uuid "00b886e2-e15e-5b9e-a371-7c8e659febb1" :question/math-academy-id "e-12947" :question/problem "![](images/dc/dc96a7656f4725f266f66c74752504b9b926b21215899cb4e62548f5c748ce4e.svg)\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is a square matrix\n\nII. $A \\cdot A^T = I$\n\nIII. $A$ is an orthogonal matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a square matrix $A$ is **orthogonal** if and only if\n\n$$\nA \\cdot A^T = A^T\\cdot A = I\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the identity matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The number of rows in $A$ is the same as the number of columns. So, it's a square matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n![](images/c7/c7cae63465ded2fe22d8ad21e22458042b3324f820ce2efcbe80df061cfecaeb.svg)\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A\\cdot A^T = I,$ our matrix is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133504 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133949 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee00be0e-f6a2-5269-a36d-1eeaf1b32344" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Product of Matrices"}] :question/id #uuid "b87bd2f0-67da-5b9d-9e21-831afa44eec8" :question/math-academy-id "e-12948" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d97da6252422d33ef758793ec93d4ef85076fbaa524215ad19c709f42005aeff.svg)\n\nFind $|\\textrm{det}(PQ)|$ given that $P$ is an orthogonal matrix." :question/worked-solution "We use the following facts: \n\n- The determinant of a product is equal to the product of the determinants.\n\n- The determinant of an orthogonal matrix $P$ is equal to $\\pm1.$\n\nApplying these ideas, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\text{det}(PQ)| & =|\\text{det}(P)\\cdot \\text{det}(Q)| \\\\  & =|(\\pm 1)\\cdot \\text{det}(Q)| \\\\  & =|\\pm 1|\\cdot |\\text{det}(Q)| \\\\  & =|\\text{det}(Q)| \\\\  & =|(-2\\sqrt{3})\\cdot 3\\sqrt{3}-2\\cdot 1| \\\\  & =|-18-2| \\\\  & =|-20| \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133505 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133950 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a22cfc-9a78-57ab-93a4-3c3255c34bf7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inverse of an Orthogonal Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "9d50c0e3-386e-57db-b509-bed0d933e6ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-12949" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the orthogonal matrix ![](images/37/37ca77e520fc9797f39e3fb8d381c359a6c7f6f251d17de6ef9586968d5d0d0b.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The inverse of an orthogonal matrix is equal to its transpose.\n\nTherefore, since $D$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{-1} & ={D}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133506 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133951 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da20ffe7-173b-5fc0-8629-3b9b70222860" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Matrices"}] :question/id #uuid "848f3901-12b3-5a37-bb03-08743e7058c8" :question/math-academy-id "e-12950" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the $n\\times n$ orthogonal matrices $P$ and $Q?$\n\nI. $2Q$ is orthogonal\n\nII. $P^{-1}Q$ is orthogonal\n\nIII. $PQ^T$ is orthogonal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ Q^T = Q^T Q = I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have $Q^{-1} = Q^T.$\n\n- $(AB)^T = B^TA^T$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the product $2Q \\cdot (2Q)^T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2Q\\cdot (2Q)^{T} & =2Q\\cdot 2{Q}^{T} \\\\  & =4Q{Q}^{T} \\\\  & =4I \\\\  & \\ne I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the product $(P^{-1}Q)\\cdot (P^{-1}Q)^T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({P}^{-1}Q)\\cdot ({P}^{-1}Q)^{T} & =({P}^{T}Q)\\cdot ({P}^{T}Q)^{T} \\\\  & ={P}^{T}Q\\cdot {Q}^{T}({P}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={P}^{T}(Q{Q}^{T})P \\\\  & ={P}^{T}(I)P \\\\  & ={P}^{T}P \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Computing the product $(PQ^T)\\cdot(PQ^T)^T ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(P{Q}^{T})\\cdot (P{Q}^{T})^{T} & =P{Q}^{T}\\cdot ({Q}^{T})^{T}{P}^{T} \\\\  & =P{Q}^{T}Q{P}^{T} \\\\  & =P({Q}^{T}Q){P}^{T} \\\\  & =P(I){P}^{T} \\\\  & =P{P}^{T} \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365031 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133950 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a22cfc-9a78-57ab-93a4-3c3255c34bf7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inverse of an Orthogonal Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e43a7a4-61dd-55ec-ac22-f5f69dc1275a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{8}{17} & \\frac{15}{17} \\\\ -\\frac{15}{17} & \\frac{8}{17}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365033} {:answer/id #uuid "fa352170-de9d-577d-8a45-161b02256251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{17} & \\frac{15}{17} \\\\ -\\frac{15}{17} & \\frac{8}{17}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365034} {:answer/id #uuid "70c31298-70c6-5e44-939c-7298d4a5a16f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{8}{17} & \\frac{15}{17} \\\\ \\frac{15}{17} & -\\frac{8}{17}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365035} {:answer/id #uuid "9a5ee8fa-6107-51dc-ac45-c48f635f9e34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D$ is not invertible" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365036} {:answer/id #uuid "4e124c1e-5d1b-539c-af6f-7ee8d04d6b79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{17} & -\\frac{15}{17} \\\\ \\frac{15}{17} & \\frac{8}{17}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e124c1e-5d1b-539c-af6f-7ee8d04d6b79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{17} & -\\frac{15}{17} \\\\ \\frac{15}{17} & \\frac{8}{17}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365037} :answer-field/id #uuid "be68f4d8-23a0-5fb9-8946-0f0a127eb8fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365032}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "799244d5-bb2f-52af-ab58-533edeceb08d" :question/math-academy-id "q-140562" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the orthogonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{17} & \\frac{15}{17} \\\\ -\\frac{15}{17} & \\frac{8}{17}\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "The inverse of an orthogonal matrix is equal to its transpose.\n\nTherefore, since $D$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{-1} & ={D}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{17} & -\\frac{15}{17} \\\\ \\frac{15}{17} & \\frac{8}{17}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133950 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a22cfc-9a78-57ab-93a4-3c3255c34bf7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inverse of an Orthogonal Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [